@flexiarg orchestration/calibrate-consent-between-overshoot-and-undershoot
@title Calibrate Consent Between Overshoot and Undershoot — scope the approval so the agent neither over-builds nor stalls
@status accepted 2026-11-11: proposed by 象 for 2 turns (Joe 2026-11-11: recurrence suffices); attested once used in a mission or a War Machine cascade
@provenance 象 proposals, ~/.emacs-graph/session-turn-analysis/<record>[.json].candidates.json; records: turn-InkU3S turn-mq3ECy; first proposed for: An agent holds an operator approval whose scope is ambiguous in both directions at once.
@why [orchestration/consent-gate]

! conclusion: Clarify the nature of the approval before acting — name what is in and out — aiming between the two failure modes, and treat this calibration as a first-class design object, not an inconvenience.

  + IF:
    A vague approval could be read too broadly (run off and build everything) or too narrowly (build a little, stop all work, re-ask).

  + HOWEVER:
    Both readings are failures: overshoot spends trust and builds unwanted things; undershoot wedges the loop on constant re-questioning, which is its own tax on the operator.

  + THEN:
    Clarify the nature of the approval before acting — name what is in and out — aiming between the two failure modes, and treat this calibration as a first-class design object, not an inconvenience.

  + BECAUSE:
    The consent-gate records the approval locus but is silent on how the operator and agent negotiate the scope of what is approved; this names the two-sided failure mode that scope clarification must steer between.
